{
  "an4.8:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 4.8 ",
  "an4.8:0.2": "1. At Wares Village ",
  "an4.8:0.3": "Self-assured ",
  "an4.8:1.1": "“Mendicants, a Realized One possesses four kinds of self-assurance. With these he claims the bull’s place, roars his lion’s roar in the assemblies, and turns the divine wheel. ",
  "an4.8:1.2": "What four? ",
  "an4.8:1.3": "I see no reason for anyone—whether ascetic, brahmin, god, Māra, or the Divinity, or anyone else in the world—to legitimately scold me, saying: ‘You claim to be a fully awakened Buddha, but you don’t understand these things.’ ",
  "an4.8:1.4": "Since I see no such reason, I live secure, fearless, and self-assured. ",
  "an4.8:2.1": "I see no reason for anyone—whether ascetic, brahmin, god, Māra, or the Divinity, or anyone else in the world—to legitimately scold me, saying: ‘You claim to have ended all defilements, but you still have these defilements.’ ",
  "an4.8:2.2": "Since I see no such reason, I live secure, fearless, and self-assured. ",
  "an4.8:3.1": "I see no reason for anyone—whether ascetic, brahmin, god, Māra, or the Divinity, or anyone else in the world—to legitimately scold me, saying: ‘The acts that you say are obstructions are not really obstructions for the one who performs them.’ ",
  "an4.8:3.2": "Since I see no such reason, I live secure, fearless, and self-assured. ",
  "an4.8:4.1": "I see no reason for anyone—whether ascetic, brahmin, god, Māra, or the Divinity, or anyone else in the world—to legitima